From: ClauCoDillo NuttX port
Subject: [PATCH] mwin: reset stuck Alt status on window refocus

src/mwin/winuser.c's TranslateMessage() tracks whether Alt is
currently held in a static bAltStatus, updated only when it sees a
matching WM_KEYDOWN/WM_KEYUP(VK_MENU) pair pass through this
process's own message queue. When Alt is believed held, an ordinary
WM_KEYDOWN is translated to WM_SYSCHAR instead of WM_CHAR -- and
cfltk's WndProc (fl_nx_event.c) only has a case for WM_CHAR, so
WM_SYSCHAR is silently dropped at DefWindowProc. Net effect when
bAltStatus gets stuck TRUE: ordinary typing does nothing at all, with
no error anywhere.

It gets stuck because Alt-Tabbing to a *different* X11 application
(then back) routinely splits a modifier's press and release across
two different windows/processes -- X11 delivers each half to
whichever window has real focus at that instant. If this process
only ever saw the press half, bAltStatus never gets cleared again.

Confirmed live via a full-pipeline trace (X11 capture -> driver read
-> mwin window-level focus -> this exact function) showing every
earlier layer correctly delivering keystrokes to the right window,
with a lone unpaired Alt-keydown sitting right before typing that
then went nowhere.

Fix: reset bAltStatus in SetFocus(), unconditionally -- including on
its "already focused" no-op early-return path. That path matters:
this process's single top-level window regaining real X11 focus
(e.g. Alt-Tab back, or clicking a taskbar icon) doesn't necessarily
change mwin's own internal focuswp at all, since there was never a
second mwin-internal window competing for it -- a caller
re-asserting focus on refocus (confirmed via trace: SetFocus() *is*
called again every time) hits exactly that no-op path. An earlier
version of this fix only reset bAltStatus after an actual focuswp
change, which still left the bug reproducible via Alt-Tab-away-and-
back-then-click-then-type, for exactly this reason.

Found and root-caused while testing the ClauCoDillo browser port:
user-reported "sometimes the keyboard just stops working after
switching windows and back," reproduced on demand via Alt-Tab and
taskbar-icon switching, confirmed fixed via repeated stress-testing
after the fix (Alt-Tab away/back and taskbar-click cycles no longer
reproduce it).
---
--- a/src/mwin/winuser.c
+++ b/src/mwin/winuser.c
@@ -242,11 +242,22 @@
 	return lpMsg->message != WM_QUIT;
 }

+/* Tracks whether Alt is currently believed held, so TranslateMessage()
+ * below knows whether a WM_KEYDOWN should become a WM_CHAR or a
+ * WM_SYSCHAR (cfltk's WndProc only handles WM_CHAR -- WM_SYSCHAR is
+ * silently dropped, so a wrong TRUE here means typing does nothing
+ * at all). File-scope (not function-local) so SetFocus() can reset it
+ * too -- see that reset site's own comment for the full story: this
+ * variable is only ever updated from a matching VK_MENU press+release
+ * pair passing through this process's own message queue, and
+ * Alt-Tabbing to a *different* X11 application (then back) routinely
+ * delivers only one half of that pair here, leaving it stuck TRUE
+ * forever without a reset hook elsewhere. */
+static BOOL bAltStatus;
+
 BOOL WINAPI
 TranslateMessage(CONST MSG *lpMsg)
 {
-	static BOOL bAltStatus;	/* store ALT status to send WM_SYSCHAR */
-
 	/* Creat the WM_CHAR on WM_KEYDOWN messages*/
    	/* if bit 24 in lParam is ON (control key), don't post WM_CHAR */
 	if(lpMsg && (lpMsg->message == WM_KEYDOWN || lpMsg->message == WM_KEYUP) &&
@@ -923,6 +934,23 @@
 	if (!IsWindow(hwnd))
 		return 0;

+	/* Reset TranslateMessage()'s bAltStatus here, unconditionally --
+	 * even on the "already focused" no-op path below. This process's
+	 * single top-level window regaining real X11 focus (e.g. after
+	 * Alt-Tabbing to a *different* X11 application and back) doesn't
+	 * necessarily change mwin's own internal focuswp at all -- there
+	 * was never a second mwin-internal window competing for it -- so
+	 * a caller re-asserting focus here (e.g. on a click) hits the
+	 * hwnd==focuswp branch below and returns early. An earlier version
+	 * of this fix only reset bAltStatus after an actual focuswp
+	 * change, which meant exactly that scenario -- Alt-Tab away and
+	 * back, then click, then type -- still left it stuck: confirmed
+	 * live via a trace showing SetFocus() being called again on every
+	 * refocus, just always landing on this no-op path instead. See
+	 * bAltStatus's own declaration above for why a stuck TRUE here
+	 * silently breaks all typing. */
+	bAltStatus = FALSE;
+
 	if(hwnd == focuswp)
 		return focuswp;
